// `std.signal` is hosted-only: the freestanding profile drops it, exactly as it
// drops `std.net` (design 272 unit 3, SL-228).
//
// Signals are an operating-system facility. A freestanding target has no kernel
// to deliver one and no process to deliver it to, so the module is ABSENT
// rather than present-and-broken — the refusal is at the import, naming the
// module, instead of a link failure against seams that do not exist.
//
// The bare-metal target is what puts the profile in play: `--freestanding`
// alone rejects the host's own triple first and the row would prove nothing
// (the lesson N05 records).
// COMPILE-FLAGS: --freestanding --target riscv32-unknown-none-elf
// EXPECT: error
// EXPECT-ERROR-CONTAINS: module `std.signal` is hosted-only and cannot be imported in the freestanding profile

import std.signal.{Signal, SignalWatch}

func main() {
    let _ = SignalWatch.watch(Signal.Terminate)
}
